evolution-jmap (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * JMAP push: the mail, calendar and address-book backends subscribe to the
    server's EventSource channel and refresh when the server announces a
    change, instead of waiting for the next poll. The folder list picks up
    mailboxes created or renamed elsewhere, the subscription follows OAuth2
    token refreshes, and repeated identical announcements are deduplicated.
  * A delivery arriving over push no longer steals the folder selection: a
    message landing in a mailbox you were not looking at used to jump the
    folder tree away from the folder you had selected. The folder list is
    now marked stale only when the tree's shape actually changed.
  * Out of Office: a vacation autoresponder page in the account editor, so
    the server's autoresponder can be switched on, dated and worded without
    leaving Evolution.
  * Send Later: the composer can hand a message to the server for delivery
    at a chosen time, on servers that offer delayed submission.
  * Snooze: the message-list context menu can put a message aside until a
    later time, on servers that offer it.
  * New Address Book / New Calendar: JMAP now appears in the Type list, and
    editing an existing JMAP book or calendar no longer crashes the dialog.
  * Folder searches the server can evaluate are answered by Email/query on
    the server instead of only the locally cached summary.
  * Folder properties now report the server's quota (RFC 9425), and a server
    that offers no quota at all is no longer reported as a server error.
  * Each account keeps its own OAuth 2.0 token slot, so two accounts with
    the same user name on different servers no longer share one token. This
    needs evolution-data-server 3.62 or newer to take effect; on 3.52 it is
    recorded and ignored.
  * Deleting a collection the server forbids deleting is greyed out instead
    of failing after the click.
  * A newly created address book or calendar keeps the name you typed; it
    could previously show the server's id as its name.
  * No sign-in window is offered when there is no keyring to keep the token;
    a PKCE consent that could never be redeemed is refused up front.
  * Contact photos whose bytes look like base64 are no longer decoded twice,
    and saving an unchanged contact or event no longer writes a spurious
    patch to the server.
  * Rebasing server-reported URLs onto the session origin is now a
    per-account setting, so a tunnelled self-hosted account and a public
    account can coexist in one process.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Thu, 03 Sep 2026 09:07:46 -0700

evolution-jmap (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the hourly OAuth2 re-consent: a long-lived backend connection kept
    an expired access token and escalated its 401 to a consent window instead
    of refreshing. All four connection paths (calendar, address book, mail
    store, mail transport) now refresh once and retry. Operator-validated
    against a real account: seven token expiries handled silently, no consent
    windows.
  * Fixed mail body downloads against real servers: a cross-origin redirect on
    the blob download is now refused rather than returned as if it were the
    message.
  * Fixed the Inbox showing "no messages" until the user switched folders and
    back.
  * A dead Evolution instance's stale OAuth2 D-Bus proxy is now reported as
    itself rather than misclassified as a keyring failure.
  * Account setup and OAuth 2.0 improvements, especially against Fastmail.
  * Calendar and address-book creation and deletion.
  * A large batch of contact and calendar field-mapping fidelity work, driven
    by real-exporter fixtures, property-based tests and mutation testing.

 -- Tobias Mueller <muelli@cryptobitch.de>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 11:36:58 -0700

